<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rígsþula</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rígsmál, The Lay of Ríg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rígsþula (The Lay of Ríg) is an Eddic poem, preserved incomplete in the 14th-century Codex Wormianus, in which a god named Ríg fathers the social classes of mankind. Visiting three couples in turn, he fathers Þræll, ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">thralls</a>, Karl, ancestor of free farmers and craftsmen, and Jarl, ancestor of the warrior nobility; Jarl's youngest son Konr ungr, skilled in runes and magic, stands for the origin of the word konungr, king. The prose introduction identifies Ríg with Heimdall, though some scholars think the role fits Odin better. Datings of the poem range from the 10th to the 13th century, and it breaks off as a crow urges Konr to seek the halls of Dan and Danþír.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rigs-ula.jpg" alt="Rígsþula" width="330" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W.G. Collingwood (1854 - 1932) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rig_in_Great-grandfather's_Cottage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Eddic poem <b>Rígsþula</b> tells how the god Ríg fathered Thrall, Karl and Jarl, the ancestors of slaves, free farmers and nobles (Viking Age Scandinavia).</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote>The Eddic poem <b>Rígsþula</b>, of uncertain date and preserved in a 14th-century manuscript, tells how the god Ríg fathered three sons, Thrall, Karl and Jarl, the ancestors of slaves, free farmers and nobles, and archaeology confirms a society divided along broadly those lines.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li></ul></section>
